<Document
  title="Privacy"
  description="What J5 Code sends from your machines, who receives it, and how to turn it off."
  updated="2026-10-24"
>
  <p>
    J5 Code is open-source software maintained by Jackson Miller. It runs on your own machines.
    There is no hosted J5 service and no J5 account, so your projects, conversations, and files
    are never sent to the maintainer. They leave your machines only when you run an agent, as
    described below.
  </p>

  <h2>Usage data</h2>
  <p>
    The J5 Code server sends usage events to PostHog, an analytics service, so the maintainer can
    see how the software is used. Events are stored in the United States and kept for 14 days.
  </p>
  <p>The events are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>the server starting, with a count of your threads and projects;</li>
    <li>
      a client connecting, with the kind of client (web, desktop, or mobile), its version, its
      operating system, the browser name on web, the device model and operating system version on
      mobile, and how it connected;
    </li>
    <li>a thread starting, and a turn being requested;</li>
    <li>
      a turn starting, with who asked for it (you, an agent, or J5 Code itself), where the request
      came from (a client, an agent tool, or the server), whether a scheduled task or a background
      wake-up started it, whether it is the thread's first turn, and whether the thread was started
      by an agent or is a seat in a Crew;
    </li>
    <li>
      an ask ending, with how it ended (answered, withdrawn, or dropped), the kind of participant
      that asked and that was asked (a person, an agent, or a script), its urgency, and how long it
      was open;
    </li>
    <li>
      a playbook run starting or ending, with the number of steps, how many steps name a persona,
      whether a Crew runs it, and at the end whether it completed or was cancelled, the step it
      reached, and how long it ran;
    </li>
    <li>
      a Crew roster being approved or declined, with the number of seats asked for and approved, how
      many seats you added, removed, renamed or changed, whether it follows a playbook, and how long
      the decision took;
    </li>
    <li>
      a Crew's seats launching, with the number of seats, how many use a persona, a worktree or full
      access, how many different models they use, and how many failed to start;
    </li>
    <li>
      every fifteen minutes, counts of your open threads, threads that are working, working threads
      an agent started, queued turns, live Crews, the seats in the largest Crew, and asks waiting on
      you and on agents;
    </li>
    <li>
      a turn finishing, with the provider, model, reasoning effort, permission mode, result,
      duration, and token totals;
    </li>
    <li>
      a feature being used, such as restoring a checkpoint, forking a thread, opening a terminal,
      or creating a worktree, with the feature's name, a fixed option where the feature has one
      (for example which kind of restore), and the kind of client used. Paths, names, IDs, and
      text are never sent;
    </li>
    <li>
      an agent calling one of J5 Code's tools, with the tool's name, the kind of provider that
      called it, whether it succeeded, a fixed error code if it failed, and how long it took.
      Calls that hand work to another thread also carry the provider, model, and modes on each
      side and the fixed settings the agent chose; other tools are reported only when a call
      fails. A model is named only when it is one of a provider's own published models;
    </li>
    <li>
      a task an agent handed to another agent ending, with how it ended, the provider and model on
      each side, and how long it took.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Each event also carries the J5 Code version, its release channel (stable, nightly, preview, or
    a development build), the server's operating system and processor architecture, and the WSL
    distribution name if there is one.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you set <code>J5CODE_TELEMETRY_TAG</code> in the server's environment, its value is sent with
    every event. It is unset unless you set it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Events are tied to an identifier: a one-way hash of your Codex account ID, or of your Claude
    user ID if there is no Codex account, or of a random ID created on your machine if there is
    neither. Each event also carries an install ID: a one-way hash of a random ID created when the
    server is installed, which stays the same for that install.
  </p>
  <p>
    To count how many installs one person runs, the server also sends an event naming who an
    install belongs to, once for each owner it sees while it runs. An owner is a one-way hash of
    one of: your T3 Connect account, your OpenAI user ID and your Claude account ID when you are
    signed in to those providers, and a random ID that each web, desktop, or mobile client creates
    for its device when it connects. Clients send the server only these hashes.
  </p>
  <p>The original IDs are never sent. IP addresses are not stored.</p>
  <p>
    Events never include prompts, responses, file contents, file or project names, authentication
    tokens, or conversation IDs.
  </p>
  <p>
    To turn this off, set <code>T3CODE_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=false</code> in the server's environment
    before starting it.
  </p>

  <h2>Other connections J5 Code makes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Update checks.</strong> J5 Code checks GitHub for new releases and downloads them
      from there. GitHub sees the request, under its own privacy policy.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your agent providers.</strong> When you run an agent, your prompts and code go to the
      provider you chose (such as OpenAI, Anthropic, or Cursor) through that provider's own tool,
      under your account and their terms. J5 Code's maintainer never receives them.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The mobile app</h2>
  <p>
    The mobile app connects to J5 Code servers you add and stores those connection details on your
    device. It contains no analytics and sends nothing to the maintainer itself. When it connects,
    it gives the server a hash of its device ID and of your T3 Connect account if you are signed in,
    and the server reports them as described under Usage data.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions or requests about this page: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>
</Document>
